===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*kelh₁-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|reconciliacioun|t=act of reconciling; state of accord or harmony;}}{{nb...|reconcyliacyoun, reconsiliation, reconsiliacion, reconsiliacioun, reconsyliacion, reconsylyacion,|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=reconciliāciǒun|pos=n|id=MED36213}}</ref> from {{der|en|xno|reconciliaciun}}, {{m|xno|reconsiliacion}}, {{m|xno|reconsiliaciun}}, and {{der|en|frm|reconciliation}}, {{m|frm|reconsiliacion}}, {{m|frm|reconsiliation|t=act of reconciling; result of this act; act of bringing about agreement or harmony; reconsecration of a desecrated place}} (modern {{cog|fr|réconciliation}}), and from their {{glossary|etymon}} {{der|en|la|reconciliātiō|t=reinstatement, renewal, restoration; reconciliation}} (compare {{cog|LL.|reconciliātiō|t=reconciliation; reconsecration of a desecrated place}}, from {{m|la|reconciliāre}} + {{m|la|-iō|pos={{glossary|suffix}} forming {{glossary|abstract noun}}s from {{glossary|verb}}s}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=159781|date=June 2009|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref> {{m|la||Reconciliāre}} is the {{glossary|present}} {{glossary|active}} {{glossary|infinitive}} of {{m|la|reconciliō|t=to bring together again, conciliate, reconcile, reunite; to bring back; to recover, re-establish, regain, restore, win back}}, from {{m|la|re-|pos={{glossary|prefix}} meaning ‘again’}} + {{m|la|conciliō|t=bring together, unite; to gain; to win over; to recommend; to procure, purchase}} (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*kelh₁-|t=to call, cry, summon}}).

